---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Rostr from "../components/Rostr.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How rostr names your sessions"
  description="How rostr uses Claude Code hooks to name each session, and why the agent writes its own title."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <h1>How <Rostr /> names your sessions</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every Claude Code session gets a name from your roster and a short summary of what it's doing, like <span
        class="session-title">🧹 Ginny · Fix flaky login test</span>. Here's how that works under the hood, and why it
      works this way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      When a session starts, <Rostr /> drafts a name from your roster, like 🧹 Ginny. During its first reply, the agent
      writes a 3-5 word summary of the task and sets the session's title to <span class="session-title"
        >🧹 Ginny · &lt;summary&gt;</span>. When the session ends, Ginny goes back to the bench for the next one.
    </p>
    <p>All of it runs on Claude Code hooks that call the <Rostr /> CLI.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The hooks</h2>
    <p><code>rostr assemble</code> installs four hooks into <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>.</p>
    <dl class="rows">
      <div class="row">
        <dt><code>SessionStart</code></dt>
        <dd>
          Records the session and sets <code>ROSTR_SESSION</code>, so any <Rostr /> command run later knows which session
          it's in.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt><code>UserPromptSubmit</code></dt>
        <dd>
          On the first prompt, drafts a name before Claude sees the prompt, tells the agent who it is as additional
          context, and asks it to title the session as part of its first reply. Later prompts keep the name alive.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt><code>PostToolUse</code></dt>
        <dd>
          Runs after a title tool, or after the agent records its task in a terminal. Records the summary from the title as the agent's task, so <code>rostr list</code> shows
          what everyone is doing. Since the title call comes after the answer, it ends the turn there, so Claude
          doesn't have to reply to the tool's result.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt><code>SessionEnd</code></dt>
        <dd>Releases the name.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Why the agent sets the title</h2>
    <p>The obvious design is for <Rostr />'s hook to set the whole title itself. Claude Code doesn't allow that today:</p>
    <ul class="terse-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Hooks can set a title at only two moments.</strong> Claude Code lets a hook set the session's title
        when the session starts or when a prompt arrives.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hook titles don't reach every session.</strong> In the desktop app, a hook's title shows up for Local
        sessions, but not for Remote Control or cloud sessions. That's a known Claude Code issue. Those sessions can
        only be renamed through a title tool.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The app's own summary lands too late.</strong> The desktop app writes its own short title about a
        second after your first message, but only saves it where other programs can read it a few seconds later, after{" "}
        <Rostr />'s first-prompt hook has already run. Nothing outside the app can make it save sooner.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      So <Rostr /> hands that part to the agent. It already knows what the task is, so it writes a 3-5 word summary and
      sets <span class="session-title">&lt;emoji name&gt; · &lt;summary&gt;</span> with the session's title tool,
      during its first reply.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Which tool sets the title</h2>
    <p>It depends on where the session runs.</p>
    <table class="surface-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where the session runs</th>
          <th>How the title is set</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Desktop app, Local session</th>
          <td>The app's own session tool: <code>set_session_title</code> with session <code>"self"</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Desktop app via Remote Control, and cloud sessions</th>
          <td>Claude Code's remote <code>set_session_title</code>, with the session's server ID</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Terminal</th>
          <td>
            There's no title tool, so the agent isn't asked. <Rostr /> summarizes your first prompt itself in the
            background (a headless Claude Code run on your own login, with Haiku), and its hook puts <span
              class="session-title">&lt;name&gt; · &lt;summary&gt;</span> in the terminal title on your next prompt
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Why there's no extra message</h2>
    <p>
      Any tool call normally makes Claude reply again, and Claude Code doesn't let a turn end with no visible text.
      Left alone, a title call would make Claude add one more message after your answer. <Rostr /> avoids that:
    </p>
    <ul class="terse-list">
      <li>
        <strong>The call comes after the answer.</strong> The agent writes its answer, then makes the title call at the
        end of that same message, whether the answer took one sentence or a long stretch of work.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Then the turn ends.</strong> <Rostr />'s PostToolUse hook sees that the answer is already written and
        nothing but the title call came after it, and ends the turn right there, so the answer stays the last thing
        on screen.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What didn't work</h2>
    <p>Each of these looked simpler, and none held up:</p>
    <ul class="terse-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Asking for the title after the answer, with a Stop hook.</strong> Claude Code shows it as a hook error,
        and the title line becomes the session's last message.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Waking Claude in the background once the app's summary exists.</strong> The wake-up only arrives after
        the reply, and applying a title from it either shows you a blocked-prompt notice or makes Claude take another
        visible turn.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reading the app's own summary.</strong> It's saved where other programs can read it seconds too late.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Generating the summary outside Claude</strong>, with a separate <code>claude -p</code> call or an
        on-device model. Slow, may cost money, and depends on the platform.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>
      One short summary written by the agent and one or two small tool calls on the first reply. No extra round trips
      for the answer, and no extra messages.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What's next</h2>
    <p>
      If Claude Code lets a hook's title reach Remote Control and cloud sessions, <Rostr /> can drop the agent's part and
      title those sessions straight from the hook.
    </p>
    <p class="docs-link"><a href="/cli">Read the full CLI docs →</a></p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-hero {
    padding: 72px 0 40px;
  }

  .page-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(36px, 6vw, 52px);
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }

  .section p {
    max-width: 40em;
  }

  .rows {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 10px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .row dt code {
    background: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .row dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 4px;
    }
  }

  .surface-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .surface-table th,
  .surface-table td {
    padding: 10px 8px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .surface-table thead th {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  .surface-table tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .surface-table td {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .terse-list {
    margin: 16px 0;
    padding-left: 20px;
    max-width: 44em;
  }

  .terse-list li {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
</style>
